\title{The proof-comparison shortcuts are admissible as a profile}
\taxon{Decision}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{performance}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{docs/design/OWNER-NOTES.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted as a policy. The shortcuts are admissible; whether they are on by default is decided by warm wall clock over all corpora and is not decided yet. Today they are off and reachable only through a system property.}

\p{\strong{Context.} [The two shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts), \code{skipargs} and \code{congruence}, stop the machine from comparing proofs the official way: by the types of proof arguments and proof fields, after unfolding both sides. They were part of the first design, were found by the soundness review to accept exports that the official checker rejects (findings S-F1 and S-F2), and were removed from every switch set under the rule of owner note 11, "resemble official by default". Three later results reopened the question:}
\ul{
\li{The profiles: on Mathlib 40 \% of the machine's instructions are this one class of work (P8), on navier-stokes-euler 54.5 \% (P9). The leading checkers of the arena have the same family of shortcuts in their sources.}
\li{A second reviewer (S2) attacked the two options for soundness \em{with respect to the theory}: every equation they assume is derivable, provided both sides of a comparison are well-typed, which inference establishes. No false claim was accepted under any configuration. The exports accepted against the official verdict are all official incompleteness: for each of the 15 known ones the official checker accepts a proof of the same statement once the congruence step is written out as a term.}
\li{A prototype (C1) looked for a way to remove the same work with official verdicts and found none: the recoverable share is zero within the resolution of its measurements.}
}

\p{\strong{Decision.} Owner note 13 (2026-11-24) sets the contract of the default configuration: never accept a statement that is not derivable; give the official verdict on every real input; accepting a derivable statement that the official checker rejects only through its own incompleteness is tolerated. Under that contract the two shortcuts are admissible as a profile. Whether the profile is the default is decided by warm wall clock over all corpora, because they cost on con-leche.}

\p{\strong{Evidence: what they are worth.}}
\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{Default switch set}\th{With the two options}\th{Conditions}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib}\td{25.39 s per warm pass}\td{19.15 s (-24.6 \%)}\td{native image, 8 workers, pipelined, passes 2 and 3 of one process, one quiet window}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib slice of algebraic geometry}\td{462 ms}\td{388 ms (-16 \%)}\td{as above, median of passes 2 to 4; a slice of this size is bounded by its pipelined load}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{54.27 s}\td{24.56 s (-54.7 \%)}\td{thread CPU time of the checks, native image, one worker, quiet cores}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{38.58 s}\td{31.77 s (-17.7 \%)}\td{as above}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{7.86 s}\td{8.30 s (+5.6 \%)}\td{as above; two default runs differ by 2 \%}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice}\td{443 ms}\td{454 ms, inside the spread of the passes}\td{warm pass, native image, 8 workers, pipelined}}
}
\p{The documents cited below hold no warm wall-clock measurement with 8 workers of the options on navier-stokes-euler, cslib, con-leche or cedar as a whole, and none on the JVM launcher; that is what the open half of the decision waits for. In the arena's own metric a dry run measured Mathlib at 1,977.0 G instructions with the options against 2,978.5 G for the machine with the switches \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} (4 workers, aarch64 development host).}

\p{\strong{Evidence: what they risk.} The adversarial review of the candidate default (the default set with \code{eager} and the two options) attacked it for a false accept only: 987 hostile exports with official verdicts, no accept of an underivable statement, every official accept accepted by the pipeline. Its two seeded faults of the shortcuts, a mask one position too wide and closures taken as equal whatever their frames hold, were invisible to the first set of rows and are caught by rows added for them. In the arena dry run the configuration with the options gives no wrong accept and no wrong reject on the 148 good and 92 bad tests; of the 30 tests of the class "either" it accepts 11 where the other configurations accept 9.}

\p{\strong{Consequences.}}
\ul{
\li{With the options, the comparison of two proofs is no longer a second check of what inference let through: soundness rests on inference alone. A profile with them should run where inference has the evidence of the differential gates, and is not a place to try inference shortcuts.}
\li{The profile accepts more statements that the official checker rejects than the default set does: the congruence rows, comparisons of the K rule that succeed by congruence, and with \code{eager} the family of the eager-reduction findings. All are of the tolerated kind and belong in the list of [divergence classes](design-official-divergence).}
\li{The strict mode never runs them: \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~} selects the reference engine.}
\li{Conditions the reviewer attached to making the profile real: named switches for the two options that turn on the behaviour of \code{facts} and \code{probes} with them (today the options do nothing without those two switches); the review's rows enter the hostile suite with their kinds; the declines of finding S3-F7 are fixed or counted; the pipeline's strict re-check stays what decides a rejection of the machine.}
}

\p{\strong{How they are selected today.} \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine -X machine.switches=default,facts,probes -Drlvm.machine.m4=congruence,skipargs~}. There is no named switch and no launcher profile yet.}

\p{Related: [practical exactness](dec-TQSI), [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract), [conversion](design-conversion), [the adversarial review method](design-adversarial-review). Detail: \src{docs/design/OWNER-NOTES.md} note 13, \src{docs/design/review/soundness-shortcuts.md}, \src{docs/design/review/exact-proof-conversion.md} section 6, \src{docs/design/review/soundness-default-set.md} section 10, \src{docs/research/profiles/P9.md} sections 4.7 and 4.8, \src{docs/research/profiles/AR1.md}. Source: [the exactness session](ses-874D) and [the profiles and prototypes](ses-JRSD).}
